2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/5a/bc/cb/b5/53444839e5d76691/riddarhusets_medalj_-_petr_original.jpg?hash=2611be50463acae60434233eb651f0e173e92691518228b9cef3128b002afd80.1795939199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL “PETR. A BJERKÉN MED. REG. ET CASTR. PRIMAR.” Nordiska museet, NM.0211297
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE A native of Stockholm, Per Af Bjerkén (1765-1818) trained in London under John Hunter and Henry Cline and served as a surgeon at St Thomas' and Guy's hospitals before his eventual return to Stockholm. Although his primary focus wa
3 CONC s general surgery, in which he was regarded as one of the leading Swedish practitioners of the time, Bjerkén also achieved notoriety as an ophthalmologist.
3 CONT
3 CONT In Sweden in 1878, a commemorative medal by Lea Ahlborn was commissioned by the Swedish Academy of Sciences in struck silver, 31 mm in diameter.
3 CONT
3 CONT The obverse depicts Bjerkén's bust facing right. The following is inscribed within the curve around: PETR. A BJERKÉN MED. REG. ET CASTR. PRIMAR.; the following is inscribed within the curve at the bottom: N. 1765 O. 1818; and the 
3 CONC artist's initials appear below the truncation of the neck: L. A.
3 CONT
3 CONT The reverse depicts a staff of Aesculapius within a laurel branch wreath tied at the bottom. The following is inscribed within the curve around: CÆCIS REDDIDIT VISUM LÆSIS VIGOREM; the following is inscribed in the exergue in 3 li
3 CONC nes: SOCIO CHIRURGOCELEBERRIMO/R. ACAD. SC. SUEC./MDCCCLXXVIII.
3 CONT
3 CONT Courtesy of: Jay M. Galst, MD, Clinical Associate Professor, New York Medical College, and Peter van Alfen, PhD, Associate Curator, American Numismatic Society.
3 CONT
3 CONT Correspondence: Dr Galst, 30 E 60th St, New York, NY 10022.
3 CONT
3 CONT ------------------------------
3 CONT
